{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "389",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s presented with a 5-week history of right facial paresis, facial numbness, and severe ipsilateral headaches. He had presented to an outside ED shortly after onset and was treated with an unknown antibiotic without steroids. Medical history included poorly controlled diabetes mellitus and hypothyroidism.\n\nFindings included:- Right-sided chemosis and exophthalmos- Purulent nasal secretions- Maxillary nerve hypoesthesia- Complete eye closure with effort- Moderate brow weakness- Lip droop with effort (House-Brackmann III right facial paresis)- Left facial nerve intact- Nasal endoscopy: polypoid changes of right middle turbinate and purulence within nasal cavity- No necrotic debris observed\n\n- Laboratory Tests:  White blood cell count at high end of normal- Imaging Studies:  CT scan findings:  1. Figure 1 (Coronal CT):      Title: Coronal Computed Tomographic Scan on Day of Presentation     Description: Orbital abscess extending from right-sided rhinosinusitis through superior maxillary sinus wall. Erosion into zygomatic bone. No neo-osteogenesis in maxillary sinuses.    2. Figure 2 (Axial CT):     Title: Axial Computed Tomographic Scan on Day of Presentation     Description: Intact lamina papyracea; disease tracking to condylar process; disease extending around mandibular ramus into parotid; disease nearly abutting styloid process.- Pathological Tests:  Findings showed chronic inflammation and fibropurulent exudate without evidence of fungus  Cultures grew coagulase-negative Staphylococcus aureus and Enterococcus faecalis",
